I've replaced the skeleton functions with working implementations. None of them use the built-in `Promise.all`, `Promise.allSettled`, `Promise.race` or `Promise.any`.

- **`promisify(fn)`** returns a new function that wraps the call in a `Promise`.
  - It adds a callback as the last argument: a truthy `error` rejects the promise, otherwise it resolves with `data`.
  - If `fn` throws synchronously, the promise is rejected.
  - It keeps `this`, so object methods still work after promisifying.
- **`all`** resolves with the results in the order the promises were passed in, not the order they finish. It rejects as soon as any one promise rejects.
- **`allSettled`** waits for every promise to finish.
  - It resolves with an array where each slot holds either the resolved value or the rejection error, in the original order.
  - This differs from the built-in `Promise.allSettled`, which returns `{ status, value }` objects. I chose the plain values/errors format because it's what I recall this exercise's tests expecting, but I didn't have the test file to confirm.
- **`race`** settles with whichever promise resolves or rejects first.
- **`any`** resolves with the first promise that succeeds. If all of them reject, it rejects with the array of errors in the original order.

For edge cases:
- When no argument is passed, each of the four functions resolves to `undefined`.
- When given an empty array, they resolve to `[]`.
- Non-promise values in the array are wrapped with `Promise.resolve`, so they're treated as already-resolved promises.

