The stub needs a full implementation. I treat it as an Eulerian circuit problem:

- **Graph model:** each number of dots is a vertex, and each domino is an undirected edge between its two halves. A closed chain that uses every domino exactly once is an Eulerian circuit of this graph.
- **When a chain exists:** every vertex must have an even degree, and all dominoes must be connected.
- **Odd degrees:** if any vertex has an odd degree, I throw `ChainNotFoundException("No domino chain found.")`.
- **Building the chain:** I use Hierholzer's algorithm, starting from the first domino's left value. Each domino is flipped where needed so that neighbouring halves match.
- **Disconnected dominoes:** if the resulting circuit doesn't contain every domino, some dominoes are disconnected, so I throw the same exception.
- **Empty input:** returns an empty chain.
- **Input list:** never modified, so immutable lists such as `Arrays.asList` work.

src/main/java/Dominoes.java
```
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

class Dominoes {

    private static final String NO_CHAIN_FOUND = "No domino chain found.";

    /*
     * Each number of dots is treated as a vertex of an undirected multigraph and each domino as
     * an edge connecting the numbers on its two halves. A closed chain using every domino exactly
     * once is then an Eulerian circuit of that graph. Such a circuit exists if and only if every
     * vertex has an even degree and all dominoes belong to a single connected component.
     */
    List<Domino> formChain(List<Domino> inputDominoes) throws ChainNotFoundException {
        if (inputDominoes.isEmpty()) {
            return new ArrayList<>();
        }

        Map<Integer, List<Integer>> dominoIndicesByValue = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < inputDominoes.size(); i++) {
            Domino domino = inputDominoes.get(i);
            dominoIndicesByValue.computeIfAbsent(domino.getLeft(), key -> new ArrayList<>()).add(i);
            dominoIndicesByValue.computeIfAbsent(domino.getRight(), key -> new ArrayList<>()).add(i);
        }

        for (List<Integer> indices : dominoIndicesByValue.values()) {
            if (indices.size() % 2 != 0) {
                throw new ChainNotFoundException(NO_CHAIN_FOUND);
            }
        }

        List<Domino> chain = findCircuit(inputDominoes, dominoIndicesByValue, inputDominoes.get(0).getLeft());

        // Dominoes that could not be reached from the starting one are disconnected from it.
        if (chain.size() != inputDominoes.size()) {
            throw new ChainNotFoundException(NO_CHAIN_FOUND);
        }

        return chain;
    }

    /*
     * Hierholzer's algorithm: keep walking along unused dominoes until getting stuck, then
     * backtrack, emitting each domino while backtracking over it. The reversed sequence of
     * emitted dominoes forms the circuit.
     */
    private List<Domino> findCircuit(List<Domino> dominoes,
                                     Map<Integer, List<Integer>> dominoIndicesByValue,
                                     int start) {
        boolean[] used = new boolean[dominoes.size()];
        Map<Integer, Iterator<Integer>> remainingByValue = new HashMap<>();
        for (Map.Entry<Integer, List<Integer>> entry : dominoIndicesByValue.entrySet()) {
            remainingByValue.put(entry.getKey(), entry.getValue().iterator());
        }

        Deque<Integer> valueStack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Deque<Domino> dominoStack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        List<Domino> circuit = new ArrayList<>();

        valueStack.push(start);
        while (!valueStack.isEmpty()) {
            int current = valueStack.peek();
            Domino next = takeUnusedDomino(current, dominoes, remainingByValue.get(current), used);
            if (next != null) {
                dominoStack.push(next);
                valueStack.push(next.getRight());
            } else {
                valueStack.pop();
                if (!dominoStack.isEmpty()) {
                    circuit.add(dominoStack.pop());
                }
            }
        }

        Collections.reverse(circuit);
        return circuit;
    }

    /*
     * Returns the next unused domino touching the given value, oriented so that its left half
     * matches that value, or null if there is none left.
     */
    private Domino takeUnusedDomino(int value,
                                    List<Domino> dominoes,
                                    Iterator<Integer> candidates,
                                    boolean[] used) {
        while (candidates.hasNext()) {
            int index = candidates.next();
            if (!used[index]) {
                used[index] = true;
                Domino domino = dominoes.get(index);
                return domino.getLeft() == value ? domino : new Domino(domino.getRight(), domino.getLeft());
            }
        }
        return null;
    }

}
```

The implementation uses backtracking:

- **Empty input:** return an empty chain.
- **Fixed start:** begin with the first domino. Every valid chain is a cycle, so any domino can be the starting point.
- **Extending the chain:** at each step, try each unused domino. If one end matches the current open end, add it, flipping it with `new Domino(right, left)` when needed. Then recurse, and undo the step if no solution follows.
- **Success check:** once every domino is used, the chain is valid only if its first left value equals its last right value.
- **No solution:** throw `ChainNotFoundException("No domino chain found.")`.

src/main/java/Dominoes.java
```
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Dominoes {

    List<Domino> formChain(List<Domino> inputDominoes) throws ChainNotFoundException {
        List<Domino> chain = new ArrayList<>();
        if (inputDominoes == null || inputDominoes.isEmpty()) {
            return chain;
        }

        int n = inputDominoes.size();
        boolean[] used = new boolean[n];

        Domino first = inputDominoes.get(0);
        used[0] = true;
        chain.add(first);

        if (search(inputDominoes, used, chain, first.getLeft(), first.getRight(), 1)) {
            return chain;
        }

        throw new ChainNotFoundException("No domino chain found.");
    }

    private boolean search(List<Domino> dominoes, boolean[] used, List<Domino> chain,
                           int start, int currentEnd, int usedCount) {
        if (usedCount == dominoes.size()) {
            return currentEnd == start;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < dominoes.size(); i++) {
            if (used[i]) {
                continue;
            }
            Domino d = dominoes.get(i);

            if (d.getLeft() == currentEnd) {
                used[i] = true;
                chain.add(d);
                if (search(dominoes, used, chain, start, d.getRight(), usedCount + 1)) {
                    return true;
                }
                chain.remove(chain.size() - 1);
                used[i] = false;
            }

            if (d.getRight() == currentEnd && d.getLeft() != d.getRight()) {
                used[i] = true;
                chain.add(new Domino(d.getRight(), d.getLeft()));
                if (search(dominoes, used, chain, start, d.getLeft(), usedCount + 1)) {
                    return true;
                }
                chain.remove(chain.size() - 1);
                used[i] = false;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }

}
