The chain must use every domino once and end where it started. That is a closed walk through a graph, so I'll solve it as a graph problem rather than by trial and error. No other files need changes.

**Approach:**
- **Graph model:** Each pip value is a point in the graph, and each domino is a link between its two values.
- **When a chain exists:** It exists only if two conditions hold:
  1. Every value appears an even number of times across all domino halves. A double such as `[3|3]` counts twice.
  2. All dominoes are connected to each other.
- **Odd count check:** If any value has an odd count, I throw `ChainNotFoundException` straight away.
- **Building the chain:** I use Hierholzer's algorithm, a standard method for walking every link in a graph exactly once. It runs in linear time, so it handles larger inputs without exponential trial-and-error.
- **Connectivity check:** If the finished chain is shorter than the input, some dominoes were in a separate group. In that case I throw the exception.
- **Domino orientation:** Each domino in the output is flipped if needed so its sides line up with its neighbours.
- **Error message:** The exception message is `"No domino chain found."`.

src/main/java/Dominoes.java
```
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

class Dominoes {

    private static final String NO_CHAIN_FOUND = "No domino chain found.";

    /**
     * Treats every pip value as a vertex and every domino as an (undirected) edge between
     * its two values. A valid closed chain that uses every domino exactly once is then an
     * Eulerian circuit of that multigraph, which exists if and only if every vertex has an
     * even degree and all dominoes belong to a single connected component.
     * The circuit is constructed with Hierholzer's algorithm in linear time.
     */
    List<Domino> formChain(List<Domino> inputDominoes) throws ChainNotFoundException {
        if (inputDominoes.isEmpty()) {
            return new ArrayList<>();
        }

        Map<Integer, Deque<Integer>> adjacency = buildAdjacency(inputDominoes);

        if (!allDegreesEven(adjacency)) {
            throw new ChainNotFoundException(NO_CHAIN_FOUND);
        }

        List<Domino> chain = findEulerianCircuit(inputDominoes, adjacency);

        // If some dominoes were never reached, they live in a disconnected component.
        if (chain.size() != inputDominoes.size()) {
            throw new ChainNotFoundException(NO_CHAIN_FOUND);
        }

        return chain;
    }

    private Map<Integer, Deque<Integer>> buildAdjacency(List<Domino> dominoes) {
        Map<Integer, Deque<Integer>> adjacency = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < dominoes.size(); i++) {
            Domino domino = dominoes.get(i);
            // A double (e.g. [3|3]) is registered twice so that it contributes 2 to the degree.
            adjacency.computeIfAbsent(domino.getLeft(), key -> new ArrayDeque<>()).add(i);
            adjacency.computeIfAbsent(domino.getRight(), key -> new ArrayDeque<>()).add(i);
        }
        return adjacency;
    }

    private boolean allDegreesEven(Map<Integer, Deque<Integer>> adjacency) {
        for (Deque<Integer> incidentDominoes : adjacency.values()) {
            if (incidentDominoes.size() % 2 != 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    private List<Domino> findEulerianCircuit(List<Domino> dominoes, Map<Integer, Deque<Integer>> adjacency) {
        boolean[] used = new boolean[dominoes.size()];
        List<Domino> chain = new ArrayList<>(dominoes.size());

        // Each stack entry holds {value, index of the domino used to reach it (-1 for the start)}.
        Deque<int[]> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(new int[] {dominoes.get(0).getLeft(), -1});

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            int[] top = stack.peek();
            int value = top[0];
            Deque<Integer> incident = adjacency.get(value);

            // Discard dominoes at this value that have already been placed in the chain.
            while (!incident.isEmpty() && used[incident.peekFirst()]) {
                incident.pollFirst();
            }

            if (!incident.isEmpty()) {
                int dominoIndex = incident.pollFirst();
                used[dominoIndex] = true;
                stack.push(new int[] {otherSide(dominoes.get(dominoIndex), value), dominoIndex});
            } else {
                stack.pop();
                int arrivedVia = top[1];
                if (arrivedVia >= 0) {
                    // Values are popped in (reversed) circuit order, so orient each domino
                    // from the popped value towards the value it was reached from.
                    Domino domino = dominoes.get(arrivedVia);
                    chain.add(new Domino(value, otherSide(domino, value)));
                }
            }
        }

        return chain;
    }

    private int otherSide(Domino domino, int value) {
        return domino.getLeft() == value ? domino.getRight() : domino.getLeft();
    }
}
```

I'll implement `formChain` with backtracking:

- **Empty input:** return an empty chain.
- **Otherwise:** start with the first domino, then repeatedly try each unused domino.
  - A domino can be placed as-is or flipped, as long as its left side matches the previous domino's right side.
- **Valid chain:** all dominoes are used and the last right value equals the first left value.
- **No chain found:** throw `ChainNotFoundException` with the message "No domino chain found."

This assumes the existing `Domino` class has a `Domino(int left, int right)` constructor and `getLeft()`/`getRight()` methods. It also assumes `ChainNotFoundException` has a `String` constructor. These are the standard versions in this exercise.

