**Design**
- **Nodes:** each node is heap-allocated with `Box` and linked through raw pointers (`Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>`).
  - `NonNull` is covariant, so `LinkedList<T>` is covariant over `T`.
  - `PhantomData<Box<Node<T>>>` tells the compiler that the list owns `T` values and drops them.
- **Safety invariants:** these are written down at the top of the file.
  - Every link points to a live node that the list owns.
  - `head` and `tail` are consistent with each other.
  - `len` equals the number of nodes.
  - Each node's `prev` and `next` links agree with its neighbours.
- **`Cursor`:** holds a `&mut LinkedList` and a pointer to its current node. While the cursor exists, it is the only way to access the list, so all changes go through it.
  - `insert_after` and `insert_before` work on an empty list, and the cursor then points at the new element.
  - `take` moves the cursor to the next element if there is one, otherwise to the previous one.
  - `next` and `prev` stay on the same element if there is no neighbour in that direction, and return `None`.
- **`Iter`:** holds a raw pointer with lifetime `'a`, tied to the list through `PhantomData<&'a T>`.
- **`Drop`:** frees every node.
- **`Send` / `Sync`:** these are implemented by hand with the same bounds the standard library uses for its owned and borrowed types.

src/lib.rs
```
// this module adds some functionality based on the required implementations
// here like: `LinkedList::pop_back` or `Clone for LinkedList<T>`
// You are free to use anything in it, but it's mainly for the test framework.
mod pre_implemented;

use std::marker::PhantomData;
use std::ptr::NonNull;

// Safety-critical invariants of `LinkedList<T>`:
//
// 1. Every `NonNull<Node<T>>` reachable from `head`, `tail` or any node's
//    `prev`/`next` was created via `Box::into_raw` (through `Box::leak`) and
//    is exclusively owned by this list. It is freed exactly once, either in
//    `Cursor::take` or in `Drop`.
// 2. `head` is `None` iff `tail` is `None` iff `len == 0`.
// 3. `head.prev` is `None`, `tail.next` is `None`.
// 4. For every node `n`: if `n.next == Some(m)` then `m.prev == Some(n)`
//    and vice versa.
// 5. `len` equals the number of nodes reachable from `head`.
// 6. A `Cursor`'s `cur` is either `None` (only when the list is empty) or
//    points to a node in its list. A cursor holds `&mut LinkedList`, so it is
//    the only way to access the list while it exists.

type Link<T> = Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>;

struct Node<T> {
    elem: T,
    prev: Link<T>,
    next: Link<T>,
}

pub struct LinkedList<T> {
    head: Link<T>,
    tail: Link<T>,
    len: usize,
    // We own `Node<T>`s (and thus `T`s); also keeps covariance over `T`.
    _marker: PhantomData<Box<Node<T>>>,
}

pub struct Cursor<'a, T> {
    list: &'a mut LinkedList<T>,
    cur: Link<T>,
}

pub struct Iter<'a, T> {
    next: Link<T>,
    _marker: PhantomData<&'a T>,
}

// Safety: LinkedList owns its `T`s just like a `Vec<T>` / `Box<T>` would.
// No shared interior mutability exists, so the usual bounds apply.
unsafe impl<T: Send> Send for LinkedList<T> {}
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Sync for LinkedList<T> {}

// Safety: Iter behaves like `&'a T`.
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Send for Iter<'_, T> {}
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Sync for Iter<'_, T> {}

// Safety: Cursor behaves like `&'a mut LinkedList<T>`.
unsafe impl<T: Send> Send for Cursor<'_, T> {}
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Sync for Cursor<'_, T> {}

impl<T> Default for LinkedList<T> {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self::new()
    }
}

impl<T> LinkedList<T> {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        LinkedList {
            head: None,
            tail: None,
            len: 0,
            _marker: PhantomData,
        }
    }

    // You may be wondering why it's necessary to have is_empty()
    // when it can easily be determined from len().
    // It's good custom to have both because len() can be expensive for some types,
    // whereas is_empty() is almost always cheap.
    // (Also ask yourself whether len() is expensive for LinkedList)
    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
        self.len == 0
    }

    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {
        self.len
    }

    /// Return a cursor positioned on the front element
    pub fn cursor_front(&mut self) -> Cursor<'_, T> {
        let cur = self.head;
        Cursor { list: self, cur }
    }

    /// Return a cursor positioned on the back element
    pub fn cursor_back(&mut self) -> Cursor<'_, T> {
        let cur = self.tail;
        Cursor { list: self, cur }
    }

    /// Return an iterator that moves from front to back
    pub fn iter(&self) -> Iter<'_, T> {
        Iter {
            next: self.head,
            _marker: PhantomData,
        }
    }
}

impl<T> Drop for LinkedList<T> {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        let mut cur = self.head.take();
        self.tail = None;
        while let Some(node) = cur {
            // SAFETY: by invariant 1 each node was allocated by Box and is
            // owned by the list; we free each exactly once while walking forward.
            let boxed = unsafe { Box::from_raw(node.as_ptr()) };
            cur = boxed.next;
        }
        self.len = 0;
    }
}

impl<T> Cursor<'_, T> {
    fn new_node(element: T, prev: Link<T>, next: Link<T>) -> NonNull<Node<T>> {
        NonNull::from(Box::leak(Box::new(Node {
            elem: element,
            prev,
            next,
        })))
    }

    /// Take a mutable reference to the current element
    pub fn peek_mut(&mut self) -> Option<&mut T> {
        // SAFETY: `cur` points to a live node of the list (invariant 6), and
        // we hold `&mut self` (thus exclusive access to the list), so handing
        // out a mutable reference tied to `&mut self` is sound.
        self.cur.map(|node| unsafe { &mut (*node.as_ptr()).elem })
    }

    /// Move one position forward (towards the back) and
    /// return a reference to the new position
    #[allow(clippy::should_implement_trait)]
    pub fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&mut T> {
        let cur = self.cur?;
        // SAFETY: `cur` points to a live node (invariant 6).
        let next = unsafe { (*cur.as_ptr()).next }?;
        self.cur = Some(next);
        self.peek_mut()
    }

    /// Move one position backward (towards the front) and
    /// return a reference to the new position
    pub fn prev(&mut self) -> Option<&mut T> {
        let cur = self.cur?;
        // SAFETY: `cur` points to a live node (invariant 6).
        let prev = unsafe { (*cur.as_ptr()).prev }?;
        self.cur = Some(prev);
        self.peek_mut()
    }

    /// Remove and return the element at the current position and move the cursor
    /// to the neighboring element that's closest to the back. This can be
    /// either the next or previous position.
    pub fn take(&mut self) -> Option<T> {
        let cur = self.cur?;
        // SAFETY: `cur` is a live node owned by the list (invariants 1, 6).
        // We unlink it, fixing neighbours/head/tail to keep invariants 2-5,
        // then reclaim its Box exactly once. No other references to the node
        // can exist since we hold `&mut` to the list.
        unsafe {
            let boxed = Box::from_raw(cur.as_ptr());
            let prev = boxed.prev;
            let next = boxed.next;

            match prev {
                Some(p) => (*p.as_ptr()).next = next,
                None => self.list.head = next,
            }
            match next {
                Some(n) => (*n.as_ptr()).prev = prev,
                None => self.list.tail = prev,
            }
            self.list.len -= 1;
            self.cur = next.or(prev);
            Some(boxed.elem)
        }
    }

    pub fn insert_after(&mut self, element: T) {
        match self.cur {
            None => {
                // Empty list (invariant 6).
                let node = Self::new_node(element, None, None);
                self.list.head = Some(node);
                self.list.tail = Some(node);
                self.cur = Some(node);
            }
            Some(cur) => {
                // SAFETY: `cur` and its `next` (if any) are live nodes of the
                // list; we link the new node in between, preserving invariants.
                unsafe {
                    let next = (*cur.as_ptr()).next;
                    let node = Self::new_node(element, Some(cur), next);
                    match next {
                        Some(n) => (*n.as_ptr()).prev = Some(node),
                        None => self.list.tail = Some(node),
                    }
                    (*cur.as_ptr()).next = Some(node);
                }
            }
        }
        self.list.len += 1;
    }

    pub fn insert_before(&mut self, element: T) {
        match self.cur {
            None => {
                // Empty list (invariant 6).
                let node = Self::new_node(element, None, None);
                self.list.head = Some(node);
                self.list.tail = Some(node);
                self.cur = Some(node);
            }
            Some(cur) => {
                // SAFETY: `cur` and its `prev` (if any) are live nodes of the
                // list; we link the new node in between, preserving invariants.
                unsafe {
                    let prev = (*cur.as_ptr()).prev;
                    let node = Self::new_node(element, prev, Some(cur));
                    match prev {
                        Some(p) => (*p.as_ptr()).next = Some(node),
                        None => self.list.head = Some(node),
                    }
                    (*cur.as_ptr()).prev = Some(node);
                }
            }
        }
        self.list.len += 1;
    }
}

impl<'a, T> Iterator for Iter<'a, T> {
    type Item = &'a T;

    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'a T> {
        let node = self.next?;
        // SAFETY: the iterator borrows the list immutably for 'a, so the node
        // is alive and not mutated for 'a; shared references are fine.
        unsafe {
            let node_ref: &'a Node<T> = &*node.as_ptr();
            self.next = node_ref.next;
            Some(&node_ref.elem)
        }
    }
}
```

The whole list lives in `src/lib.rs`. These are the changes, step by step:

1. **Data structure:** Each element is stored in a `Node<T>` allocated on the heap with `Box`. The list and nodes point to each other with `Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>`.
   - `NonNull` is covariant over `T`, so `LinkedList<T>` is covariant over `T` (Step 5).
   - `PhantomData<Box<Node<T>>>` tells the compiler that the list owns its `T` values. This matters for drop checking.
2. **Invariants:** I documented these on the types:
   - The list owns every node, and each node was created with `Box::leak`.
   - The links are consistent: `head`, `tail`, `prev`/`next` and `len` all agree.
   - A cursor's position is `None` exactly when the list is empty.
3. **Cursor:** All changes to the list go through the cursor (`take`, `insert_after`, `insert_before`). The `push_*` and `pop_*` methods in `pre_implemented` keep working through it.
   - `next` and `prev` stay on the current element if there is no neighbour in that direction.
   - Inserting into an empty list places the cursor on the new element.
4. **Iterator:** `Iter` walks the list from front to back. Its lifetime is tied to the borrow of the list.
5. **`Drop`:** This frees all nodes.
6. **`Send` and `Sync`:** These are implemented manually for the list, the cursor and the iterator. The bounds are the same as for `Box<T>`, `&mut LinkedList<T>` and `&T`.

src/lib.rs
```
// this module adds some functionality based on the required implementations
// here like: `LinkedList::pop_back` or `Clone for LinkedList<T>`
// You are free to use anything in it, but it's mainly for the test framework.
mod pre_implemented;

use std::marker::PhantomData;
use std::ptr::NonNull;

type Link<T> = Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>;

/// A single heap-allocated node of the list.
///
/// Nodes are always created via `Box::new` and turned into raw pointers with
/// `Box::leak`, so they may be reclaimed with `Box::from_raw` exactly once.
struct Node<T> {
    element: T,
    prev: Link<T>,
    next: Link<T>,
}

/// A doubly linked list.
///
/// Safety invariants (upheld by every method that mutates the list):
/// 1. Every node reachable from `head` (via `next`) or from `tail` (via `prev`)
///    is a valid, uniquely owned allocation created by `Box::leak`, owned by
///    this list and by nothing else.
/// 2. `head` is `None` iff `tail` is `None` iff `len == 0`.
/// 3. `head.prev` is `None`, `tail.next` is `None`, and for every node `n`,
///    `n.next.prev == n` and `n.prev.next == n` (links are consistent).
/// 4. `len` equals the number of nodes in the list.
///
/// Using `NonNull` (covariant) and `PhantomData<Box<Node<T>>>` makes the list
/// covariant over `T` and tells the drop checker that we own `T` values.
pub struct LinkedList<T> {
    head: Link<T>,
    tail: Link<T>,
    len: usize,
    _marker: PhantomData<Box<Node<T>>>,
}

/// A cursor pointing at an element of the list.
///
/// Invariant: `cur` is `None` iff the list is empty; otherwise it points to a
/// valid node owned by `list`. The cursor holds a unique borrow of the list,
/// so no other access to the nodes can happen while it exists.
pub struct Cursor<'a, T> {
    cur: Link<T>,
    list: &'a mut LinkedList<T>,
}

/// An iterator over shared references to the list's elements.
///
/// Invariant: `next` is either `None` or points to a valid node of a list
/// that is borrowed immutably for `'a`, so the node outlives `'a` and is
/// not mutated during that time.
pub struct Iter<'a, T> {
    next: Link<T>,
    _marker: PhantomData<&'a T>,
}

impl<T> LinkedList<T> {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        LinkedList {
            head: None,
            tail: None,
            len: 0,
            _marker: PhantomData,
        }
    }

    // You may be wondering why it's necessary to have is_empty()
    // when it can easily be determined from len().
    // It's good custom to have both because len() can be expensive for some types,
    // whereas is_empty() is almost always cheap.
    // (Also ask yourself whether len() is expensive for LinkedList)
    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
        self.len == 0
    }

    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {
        self.len
    }

    /// Return a cursor positioned on the front element
    pub fn cursor_front(&mut self) -> Cursor<'_, T> {
        Cursor {
            cur: self.head,
            list: self,
        }
    }

    /// Return a cursor positioned on the back element
    pub fn cursor_back(&mut self) -> Cursor<'_, T> {
        Cursor {
            cur: self.tail,
            list: self,
        }
    }

    /// Return an iterator that moves from front to back
    pub fn iter(&self) -> Iter<'_, T> {
        Iter {
            next: self.head,
            _marker: PhantomData,
        }
    }
}

impl<T> Drop for LinkedList<T> {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        let mut link = self.head.take();
        self.tail = None;
        while let Some(node) = link {
            // SAFETY: by invariant 1 every node was created by `Box::leak` and
            // is owned solely by this list. We walk each node exactly once and
            // never touch it again after reclaiming it.
            let boxed = unsafe { Box::from_raw(node.as_ptr()) };
            link = boxed.next;
            self.len -= 1;
        }
    }
}

// SAFETY: the list owns its `T` values just like a `Box<T>` / `Vec<T>` would,
// and there is no shared interior state; sending the list sends the `T`s.
unsafe impl<T: Send> Send for LinkedList<T> {}
// SAFETY: `&LinkedList<T>` only gives out `&T` (via `iter`), so sharing it is
// safe when `T: Sync`.
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Sync for LinkedList<T> {}

// SAFETY: a cursor is equivalent to `&mut LinkedList<T>` which is `Send`
// when `T: Send`.
unsafe impl<T: Send> Send for Cursor<'_, T> {}
// SAFETY: `&Cursor` gives no access to elements at all; being equivalent to
// `&&mut LinkedList<T>`, it is `Sync` when `T: Sync`.
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Sync for Cursor<'_, T> {}

// SAFETY: `Iter` is equivalent to `&T`, which is `Send`/`Sync` when `T: Sync`.
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Send for Iter<'_, T> {}
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Sync for Iter<'_, T> {}

// the cursor is expected to act as if it is at the position of an element
// and it also has to work with and be able to insert into an empty list.
impl<T> Cursor<'_, T> {
    /// Take a mutable reference to the current element
    pub fn peek_mut(&mut self) -> Option<&mut T> {
        // SAFETY: by the cursor invariant `cur` (if Some) points to a valid
        // node owned by the uniquely borrowed list. The returned reference
        // borrows `self` mutably, so no other access can alias it.
        self.cur.map(|node| unsafe { &mut (*node.as_ptr()).element })
    }

    /// Move one position forward (towards the back) and
    /// return a reference to the new position
    #[allow(clippy::should_implement_trait)]
    pub fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&mut T> {
        let cur = self.cur?;
        // SAFETY: `cur` points to a valid node of the list (cursor invariant).
        let next = unsafe { (*cur.as_ptr()).next };
        match next {
            Some(_) => {
                self.cur = next;
                self.peek_mut()
            }
            // Stay at the last element; there is no next position.
            None => None,
        }
    }

    /// Move one position backward (towards the front) and
    /// return a reference to the new position
    pub fn prev(&mut self) -> Option<&mut T> {
        let cur = self.cur?;
        // SAFETY: `cur` points to a valid node of the list (cursor invariant).
        let prev = unsafe { (*cur.as_ptr()).prev };
        match prev {
            Some(_) => {
                self.cur = prev;
                self.peek_mut()
            }
            // Stay at the first element; there is no previous position.
            None => None,
        }
    }

    /// Remove and return the element at the current position and move the cursor
    /// to the neighboring element that's closest to the back. This can be
    /// either the next or previous position.
    pub fn take(&mut self) -> Option<T> {
        let node = self.cur?;
        // SAFETY: `node` is a valid node created by `Box::leak` and owned by
        // the list. We unlink it from its neighbours (which are valid by
        // invariant 3) and from head/tail before reclaiming it, so no dangling
        // pointer to it remains in the list. It is reclaimed exactly once.
        unsafe {
            let boxed = Box::from_raw(node.as_ptr());
            match boxed.prev {
                Some(p) => (*p.as_ptr()).next = boxed.next,
                None => self.list.head = boxed.next,
            }
            match boxed.next {
                Some(n) => (*n.as_ptr()).prev = boxed.prev,
                None => self.list.tail = boxed.prev,
            }
            self.list.len -= 1;
            // Keeps the cursor invariant: None only if the list is now empty.
            self.cur = boxed.next.or(boxed.prev);
            Some(boxed.element)
        }
    }

    pub fn insert_after(&mut self, element: T) {
        let new = Box::new(Node {
            element,
            prev: self.cur,
            next: None,
        });
        let new_ptr = NonNull::from(Box::leak(new));
        match self.cur {
            None => {
                // Cursor invariant: `cur == None` means the list is empty.
                self.list.head = Some(new_ptr);
                self.list.tail = Some(new_ptr);
                self.cur = Some(new_ptr);
            }
            Some(cur) => {
                // SAFETY: `cur` and its `next` neighbour (if any) are valid
                // nodes of the uniquely borrowed list; `new_ptr` is a fresh
                // allocation. We relink so that invariant 3 holds again.
                unsafe {
                    let next = (*cur.as_ptr()).next;
                    (*new_ptr.as_ptr()).next = next;
                    (*cur.as_ptr()).next = Some(new_ptr);
                    match next {
                        Some(n) => (*n.as_ptr()).prev = Some(new_ptr),
                        None => self.list.tail = Some(new_ptr),
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        self.list.len += 1;
    }

    pub fn insert_before(&mut self, element: T) {
        let new = Box::new(Node {
            element,
            prev: None,
            next: self.cur,
        });
        let new_ptr = NonNull::from(Box::leak(new));
        match self.cur {
            None => {
                // Cursor invariant: `cur == None` means the list is empty.
                self.list.head = Some(new_ptr);
                self.list.tail = Some(new_ptr);
                self.cur = Some(new_ptr);
            }
            Some(cur) => {
                // SAFETY: `cur` and its `prev` neighbour (if any) are valid
                // nodes of the uniquely borrowed list; `new_ptr` is a fresh
                // allocation. We relink so that invariant 3 holds again.
                unsafe {
                    let prev = (*cur.as_ptr()).prev;
                    (*new_ptr.as_ptr()).prev = prev;
                    (*cur.as_ptr()).prev = Some(new_ptr);
                    match prev {
                        Some(p) => (*p.as_ptr()).next = Some(new_ptr),
                        None => self.list.head = Some(new_ptr),
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        self.list.len += 1;
    }
}

impl<'a, T> Iterator for Iter<'a, T> {
    type Item = &'a T;

    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'a T> {
        self.next.map(|node| {
            // SAFETY: by the `Iter` invariant, `node` is valid and the list is
            // immutably borrowed for `'a`, so handing out `&'a T` is sound.
            let node: &'a Node<T> = unsafe { &*node.as_ptr() };
            self.next = node.next;
            &node.element
        })
    }
}
```

Below is the full implementation: a doubly linked list built on `NonNull` raw pointers, with an iterator, a cursor, a `Drop` impl, and the covariance plus `Send`/`Sync` guarantees from Step 5.

**Invariants**
- Every node is allocated with `Box` and owned exclusively by the list.
- `head`, `tail` and `len` always stay consistent with the node links.
- The cursor holds `&mut LinkedList`, so nothing else can touch the list while the cursor exists.
- An empty list has `cur == None`.

**Variance**
- `NonNull<Node<T>>` is covariant, so `LinkedList<T>` is covariant over `T`.
- The `PhantomData<Box<Node<T>>>` field marks that the list owns `T` values.

**Send / Sync**
- These are implemented manually, with the same bounds as `Box<T>`.

