The list is implemented with raw `NonNull` pointers. All changes go in `src/lib.rs`.

**Data layout**
- **`Node<T>`:** each node is a separate heap allocation created with `Box`. It holds the element plus `prev` and `next` links.
- **`LinkedList<T>`:** owns all its nodes and stores `front`, `back` and `len`.
  - Storing `len` makes `len()` cheap: O(1).
  - The fields are `Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>` plus a `PhantomData<Box<Node<T>>>`. This makes the list covariant over `T`. It also tells drop-check that the list owns and drops `T` values.
- **`Cursor<'a, T>`:** holds `&'a mut LinkedList<T>` and an optional pointer to the current node. It is `None` only when the list is empty.
- **`Iter<'a, T>`:** holds a pointer to the next node and a `PhantomData<&'a T>`, so it behaves like a shared borrow of the list.

**Mutation**
- All changes to the list go through the cursor (`insert_after`, `insert_before`, `take`).
- Inserting into an empty list positions the cursor on the new element.
- Moving past either end returns `None` and leaves the cursor where it was.
- `take` moves the cursor to the next element if there is one, otherwise to the previous one.

**`Drop`**
- `Drop` repeatedly calls `take` on a front cursor. This frees every node in a loop rather than by recursion, so very long lists are fine.

**`Send` / `Sync`**
- These are implemented manually, under the same conditions as `Box<T>` / `Vec<T>`:
  - The list is `Send` if `T: Send`.
  - The list is `Sync` if `T: Sync`.
- The cursor and iterator get the same rules as `&mut LinkedList<T>` and `&T` respectively.

The safety invariants are documented at the top of the file, and every `unsafe` block has a comment explaining why it is sound.

