No external changes are needed. The only work is implementing the five functions in `promises.js`.

- **`promisify(fn)`** returns a new function that returns a `Promise`. It calls `fn` with the given arguments plus a Node-style `(error, result)` callback. If `error` is set, the promise rejects with it; otherwise it resolves with `result`. It also keeps `this`, so it works on methods.
- **`all(promises)`** resolves with an array of values in input order once every promise resolves. It rejects as soon as any one rejects.
- **`allSettled(promises)`** waits until every promise settles. It then resolves with an array holding each resolved value or rejection reason, in input order.
- **`race(promises)`** settles the same way as whichever promise settles first.
- **`any(promises)`** resolves with the first value to resolve. If every promise rejects, it rejects with an array of all the errors, in input order.

Edge cases:
- With no argument, each function resolves to `undefined`.
- With an empty array, each function resolves to `[]`.

Plain values in the input are wrapped with `Promise.resolve`, so they are accepted too. None of the built-in combinators (`Promise.all`, `allSettled`, `race`, `any`) are used.

