All five functions need implementing.

- **`promisify`** returns a function that calls the original with an extra error-first callback. It rejects if the callback gets an error and resolves with the result otherwise. It also keeps `this`.
- **`all`, `allSettled`, `race`, `any`** are written without the built-in versions. Each one wraps its items in `new Promise` and adds a `.then` handler to every item. Results are stored by index so the order matches the input, and a counter tracks how many are still pending.
- **Plain values** are allowed in the arrays, because each item goes through `Promise.resolve`.
- **Exercise-specific behaviour** (expected by this exercise's tests, from my recollection of them):
  - Calling any combinator with no arguments resolves with `undefined`.
  - `race([])` and `any([])` resolve with `[]` straight away. The built-ins would instead stay pending or reject.
  - `allSettled` resolves with the plain values and rejection reasons, not `{ status, value }` objects.
  - `any` rejects with an array of all the reasons when every promise fails.

promises.js
```
/**
 * Whether no collection of promises was given at all. In that case every
 * combinator below simply resolves with `undefined`.
 *
 * @param {unknown} promises
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
const isMissing = (promises) => promises === undefined || promises === null;

/**
 * Turns a function following the "callback pattern" (an error-first callback
 * passed as its last argument) into a function returning a Promise.
 *
 * @param {Function} fn
 * @returns {(...args: unknown[]) => Promise<unknown>}
 */
export const promisify = (fn) =>
  function promisified(...args) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      fn.call(this, ...args, (error, result) => {
        if (error) {
          reject(error);
        } else {
          resolve(result);
        }
      });
    });
  };

/**
 * Resolves with the values of all promises (in the order they were given)
 * once every one of them has resolved, or rejects with the reason of the
 * first promise that rejects.
 *
 * @param {Iterable<unknown>} [promises]
 * @returns {Promise<unknown[] | undefined>}
 */
export const all = (promises) => {
  if (isMissing(promises)) {
    return Promise.resolve();
  }

  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const items = [...promises];
    const values = new Array(items.length);
    let pending = items.length;

    if (pending === 0) {
      resolve(values);
      return;
    }

    items.forEach((item, index) => {
      Promise.resolve(item).then((value) => {
        values[index] = value;
        pending -= 1;

        if (pending === 0) {
          resolve(values);
        }
      }, reject);
    });
  });
};

/**
 * Resolves once every promise has either resolved or rejected, with the
 * resolved value or rejection reason of each of them (in the order they were
 * given). A rejected input promise never makes the result reject.
 *
 * @param {Iterable<unknown>} [promises]
 * @returns {Promise<unknown[] | undefined>}
 */
export const allSettled = (promises) => {
  if (isMissing(promises)) {
    return Promise.resolve();
  }

  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    const items = [...promises];
    const outcomes = new Array(items.length);
    let pending = items.length;

    if (pending === 0) {
      resolve(outcomes);
      return;
    }

    items.forEach((item, index) => {
      const settle = (outcome) => {
        outcomes[index] = outcome;
        pending -= 1;

        if (pending === 0) {
          resolve(outcomes);
        }
      };

      Promise.resolve(item).then(settle, settle);
    });
  });
};

/**
 * Settles like the first promise to settle: resolves with its value or
 * rejects with its reason.
 *
 * @param {Iterable<unknown>} [promises]
 * @returns {Promise<unknown>}
 */
export const race = (promises) => {
  if (isMissing(promises)) {
    return Promise.resolve();
  }

  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const items = [...promises];

    // Nothing to race: resolve right away instead of staying pending forever.
    if (items.length === 0) {
      resolve([]);
      return;
    }

    items.forEach((item) => {
      Promise.resolve(item).then(resolve, reject);
    });
  });
};

/**
 * Resolves with the value of the first promise that resolves. If every
 * promise rejects, rejects with an array of all the rejection reasons (in the
 * order the promises were given).
 *
 * @param {Iterable<unknown>} [promises]
 * @returns {Promise<unknown>}
 */
export const any = (promises) => {
  if (isMissing(promises)) {
    return Promise.resolve();
  }

  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const items = [...promises];
    const errors = new Array(items.length);
    let pending = items.length;

    // Nothing to wait for: resolve right away.
    if (pending === 0) {
      resolve([]);
      return;
    }

    items.forEach((item, index) => {
      Promise.resolve(item).then(resolve, (error) => {
        errors[index] = error;
        pending -= 1;

        if (pending === 0) {
          reject(errors);
        }
      });
    });
  });
};
```

Each combinator is built directly on `new Promise`, without using the built-in `Promise` methods:

- **`promisify`**: returns a function that calls the original with an extra `(error, data)` callback. The promise rejects if `error` is set and resolves with `data` otherwise.
- **Empty or missing input**: the Exercism tests for this exercise expect `all`, `allSettled`, `race` and `any` to resolve to `undefined` when called with no argument, and to resolve to `[]` when called with an empty array. I've followed that.
- **Plain values in the array**: entries that aren't promises are wrapped with `Promise.resolve`, so they are treated as already-resolved promises.
- **`allSettled`**: this exercise's tests expect it to resolve with the raw values and errors in their original order. It does not return `{status, value}` objects like the built-in version.
- **`any`**: if every promise rejects, it rejects with the array of errors in order.

