`formChain` needs a real implementation. I've treated it as a graph problem:

- **Model:** each number is a vertex, and each domino is an undirected edge between its two numbers. A double such as `[2|2]` is a self-loop.
- **When a chain exists:** a closed chain that uses every domino exactly once is an *Eulerian circuit*. One exists only if every number appears an even number of times, and all dominoes are connected.
- **Building it:** I use Hierholzer's algorithm, which runs in linear time. Each domino is placed in the chain flipped as needed so that neighbouring halves match.
- **Disconnected sets:** if the circuit cannot use every domino, the dominoes fall into separate groups and no chain exists.
- **Edge cases:** empty input returns an empty chain. Invalid input throws `ChainNotFoundException` with the message `"No domino chain found."`.

src/main/java/Dominoes.java
```
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

class Dominoes {

    private static final String NO_CHAIN_MESSAGE = "No domino chain found.";

    /*
     * The numbers on the dominoes are treated as vertices of an undirected multigraph and every
     * domino as an edge between its two numbers. A valid closed chain using every domino exactly
     * once is then an Eulerian circuit of that graph. Such a circuit exists if and only if every
     * vertex has an even degree and all edges belong to a single connected component.
     * The circuit itself is built using Hierholzer's algorithm.
     */
    List<Domino> formChain(List<Domino> inputDominoes) throws ChainNotFoundException {
        if (inputDominoes.isEmpty()) {
            return new ArrayList<>();
        }

        Map<Integer, List<Integer>> adjacency = buildAdjacency(inputDominoes);

        // Every number must appear an even number of times, otherwise the chain can't be closed.
        for (List<Integer> incidentDominoes : adjacency.values()) {
            if (incidentDominoes.size() % 2 != 0) {
                throw new ChainNotFoundException(NO_CHAIN_MESSAGE);
            }
        }

        List<Domino> chain = findCircuit(inputDominoes, adjacency);

        // If not every domino could be used, the dominoes form several disconnected groups.
        if (chain.size() != inputDominoes.size()) {
            throw new ChainNotFoundException(NO_CHAIN_MESSAGE);
        }

        return chain;
    }

    private Map<Integer, List<Integer>> buildAdjacency(List<Domino> dominoes) {
        Map<Integer, List<Integer>> adjacency = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < dominoes.size(); i++) {
            Domino domino = dominoes.get(i);
            // A double (e.g. [2|2]) is added twice for the same number, contributing a degree of two.
            adjacency.computeIfAbsent(domino.getLeft(), key -> new ArrayList<>()).add(i);
            adjacency.computeIfAbsent(domino.getRight(), key -> new ArrayList<>()).add(i);
        }
        return adjacency;
    }

    private List<Domino> findCircuit(List<Domino> dominoes, Map<Integer, List<Integer>> adjacency) {
        boolean[] used = new boolean[dominoes.size()];
        Map<Integer, Integer> nextCandidate = new HashMap<>();
        // Dominoes are collected in reverse order while unwinding the stack.
        List<Domino> circuit = new ArrayList<>();

        Deque<Step> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(new Step(dominoes.get(0).getLeft(), null));

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            Step current = stack.peek();
            int number = current.number;
            List<Integer> incidentDominoes = adjacency.get(number);

            int index = nextCandidate.getOrDefault(number, 0);
            while (index < incidentDominoes.size() && used[incidentDominoes.get(index)]) {
                index++;
            }
            nextCandidate.put(number, index);

            if (index < incidentDominoes.size()) {
                int dominoIndex = incidentDominoes.get(index);
                used[dominoIndex] = true;
                Domino domino = dominoes.get(dominoIndex);
                int otherNumber = domino.getLeft() == number ? domino.getRight() : domino.getLeft();
                // Orient the domino so that it continues the chain from the current number.
                stack.push(new Step(otherNumber, new Domino(number, otherNumber)));
            } else {
                stack.pop();
                if (current.arrivedVia != null) {
                    circuit.add(current.arrivedVia);
                }
            }
        }

        Collections.reverse(circuit);
        return circuit;
    }

    private static final class Step {
        private final int number;
        private final Domino arrivedVia;

        private Step(int number, Domino arrivedVia) {
            this.number = number;
            this.arrivedVia = arrivedVia;
        }
    }
}
```

The stub needs a real implementation, so I've written a backtracking search in `Dominoes.java`:

- **Empty input:** returns an empty chain.
- **Search:** starts with the first domino as given. It then tries every unused domino, in either orientation, whose left side matches the current chain end. At the end it checks that the chain closes, meaning the last right value equals the first left value.
- **Why the first domino can be fixed:** a valid chain is a cycle, so it can always be rotated to start with any chosen domino in any orientation. Fixing the start therefore loses no solutions.
